/* Prophecy (18 entries) */
DEF_ADD([
{
  id: "messianic-prophecy",
  cat: "prophecy",
  title: "Jesus Fulfilled Hundreds of Messianic Prophecies",
  who: "Matthew's Gospel ('this was to fulfill what was spoken by the prophet'); Josh McDowell; Michael Brown, Answering Jewish Objections to Jesus (2000–2010)",
  claim: `The Old Testament contains over 300 prophecies about the Messiah: his birth in Bethlehem (Micah 5:2), birth to a virgin (Isaiah 7:14), suffering and death for sins (Isaiah 53), pierced hands and feet (Psalm 22), betrayal for 30 pieces of silver (Zechariah 11:12), and his timing (Daniel 9). Jesus fulfilled them all. That's impossible by chance, so Jesus is the Messiah.`,
  short: `Almost none of these passages, read in context, is a prediction about a future messiah. Most are about events in the writer's own time. The New Testament authors, especially Matthew, read their scriptures through Jesus and found "fulfillments" by quoting lines out of context, using the Greek translation where it differed from the Hebrew, and in some cases citing verses that don't exist. Meanwhile, the things Jewish scripture actually expected the messiah to do (restore Israel, rebuild the Temple, gather the exiles, bring world peace) did not happen. That's why most Jews then and since have not accepted Jesus as the messiah.`,
  points: [
    { h: "Matthew's method: reading backward", t: `Matthew 2:15 quotes Hosea 11:1, "Out of Egypt I called my son," as fulfilled by the infant Jesus's return from Egypt. Hosea's verse reads: "When Israel was a child, I loved him, and out of Egypt I called my son. The more I called them, the more they went from me; they kept sacrificing to the Baals." It's about Israel and the Exodus, and it describes a past event, not a prediction. Matthew 2:17–18 applies Jeremiah 31:15 (Rachel weeping for her children) to Herod's massacre of infants, but in Jeremiah Rachel weeps for the exiles, and God says "they shall come back from the land of the enemy" (31:16).` },
    { h: "Prophecies that don't exist or are misattributed", t: `Matthew 2:23: Jesus lived in Nazareth "so that what had been spoken through the prophets might be fulfilled, 'He will be called a Nazorean.'" No such prophecy exists in the Hebrew Bible. Nazareth is never mentioned there. Matthew 27:9–10 attributes a prophecy about thirty pieces of silver to Jeremiah. It's from Zechariah 11:12–13, loosely combined with elements from Jeremiah.` },
    { h: "Fulfillment by staging and misreading", t: `Some fulfillments could be deliberately enacted by Jesus or arranged by the narrators: riding into Jerusalem on a donkey (Zechariah 9:9). Matthew 21:2–7 misreads Zechariah's Hebrew parallelism ("on a donkey, on a colt, the foal of a donkey," which is one animal described twice) as two animals, and has Jesus ride both at once. Mark, Luke, and John have one. A prophecy that can be "fulfilled" by anyone who reads it and acts accordingly proves nothing.` },
    { h: "The real messianic expectations weren't fulfilled", t: `Hebrew scripture expects the messiah, a human king from David's line, to restore the kingdom of Israel (Jeremiah 23:5–6; Ezekiel 37:24–25); gather the exiles (Isaiah 11:11–12); rebuild the Temple (Ezekiel 37:26–28); and bring universal peace, so that "nation shall not lift up sword against nation" (Isaiah 2:4; Micah 4:3), with everyone knowing God (Jeremiah 31:34; Zechariah 14:9). None of this happened. Christian theology moved these to a "second coming," but that was a later solution to a failed expectation.` },
    { h: "A suffering messiah wasn't expected", t: `Mainstream Jewish expectation in Jesus' time did not include a messiah who would be executed. The disciples' shock at Jesus' predictions of his death (Mark 8:31–33) and their despair after it (Luke 24:21, "we had hoped he was the one to redeem Israel") show they didn't read Isaiah 53 or Psalm 22 as predicting a dying messiah. Paul calls a crucified messiah "a stumbling block to Jews" (1 Corinthians 1:23). Christians applied these texts to Jesus after his death to make sense of it.` },
    { h: "The '300 prophecies' count", t: `Lists of "300 prophecies" include verses that aren't prophecies (Hosea 11:1), generic events (that the messiah would be a descendant of Abraham), fulfillments recorded only in the Gospels themselves, and passages whose messianic reading is disputed. The Gospels were written by believers who knew the scriptures and shaped their narratives to match them. "Prophecy historicized," in John Dominic Crossan's phrase, rather than "history remembered."` }
  ],
  replies: [
    { q: "Jewish rabbis used to read Isaiah 53 as messianic but changed their interpretation to avoid Jesus.", a: `Some later rabbinic texts (e.g., parts of the Talmud and some medieval works) do read parts of Isaiah 53 messianically, while others read it as Israel, and the reading as Israel is attested early too (Origen, *Against Celsus* 1.55, reports Jews in the 3rd century reading it as the nation). More importantly, the text itself repeatedly names the servant as Israel (Isaiah 41:8; 44:1; 49:3). See [[isaiah-53]].`, k: "rabbis isaiah 53 messianic changed interpretation avoid jesus rashi" },
    { q: "The probability of one man fulfilling just 8 prophecies is 1 in 10^17.", a: `Those numbers (from Peter Stoner, 1958) were invented, not calculated from data, and they treat the "fulfillments" as independent events reported by neutral observers. They were reported by believers writing decades later, who chose which texts to apply. See [[prophecy-odds]].`, k: "probability eight prophecies 10^17 stoner odds" }
  ],
  sources: [
    "Brown, R. E. (1993). The Birth of the Messiah, rev. ed. Doubleday, on Matthew's formula quotations.",
    "Fredriksen, P. (1999). Jesus of Nazareth, King of the Jews. Knopf.",
    "Collins, J. J. (2010). The Scepter and the Star: Messianism in Light of the Dead Sea Scrolls, 2nd ed. Eerdmans.",
    "Levine, A.-J. & Brettler, M. Z. (2020). The Bible With and Without Jesus: How Jews and Christians Read the Same Stories Differently. HarperOne.",
    "Crossan, J. D. (1995). Who Killed Jesus? HarperSanFrancisco.",
    "Origen. Against Celsus 1.55."
  ],
  keys: "messianic prophecy prophecies fulfilled 300 prophecies jesus fulfilled old testament predicted messiah matthew fulfillment out of egypt nazarene",
  rel: ["isaiah-7-14", "isaiah-53", "psalm-22", "micah-5-2", "daniel-9", "prophecy-odds"]
},
{
  id: "prophecy-odds",
  cat: "prophecy",
  title: "The Odds of Fulfilled Prophecy Are Astronomical",
  who: "Peter Stoner, Science Speaks (1958); Josh McDowell, Evidence That Demands a Verdict; Lee Strobel",
  claim: `Peter Stoner calculated that the probability of one man fulfilling just eight messianic prophecies by chance is 1 in 10^17, which is like covering Texas two feet deep in silver dollars and picking the marked one blindfolded on the first try. For 48 prophecies, the odds are 1 in 10^157. Jesus fulfilled far more.`,
  short: `The numbers are guesses dressed as calculations. They assume each "prophecy" was a genuine prediction, that each fulfillment is independently documented, and that the events are independent. None of these holds. The fulfillments are reported by the same believers who chose the texts and told the stories, often decades later. You can produce equally impressive odds for any well-known figure whose biography was written to match expectations.`,
  points: [
    { h: "The inputs are made up", t: `Stoner assigned a probability to each event (for example, 1 in 10^5 for being betrayed for 30 pieces of silver) with no data behind it. Multiplying unsupported guesses produces an unsupported product, however large.` },
    { h: "Reported fulfillment is not observed fulfillment", t: `The probability calculation should be: how likely is it that *texts written by believers* would *report* that Jesus fulfilled these prophecies? Very likely. The Gospel writers knew the scriptures and were explicitly writing to show fulfillment ("this took place to fulfill..."). Some events were simply narrated to match (see [[psalm-22]]).` },
    { h: "The prophecies aren't independent", t: `"Born in Bethlehem," "of David's line," and "a ruler from Judah" are linked: if you believe someone is the Davidic messiah, you'll expect all of them. The betrayal for silver and the purchase of the potter's field come from one passage. Multiplying the probabilities of linked events vastly overstates the improbability.` },
    { h: "Many aren't predictions at all", t: `Hosea 11:1, Jeremiah 31:15, and Psalm 22 are not predictions (see [[messianic-prophecy]]). Treating any verse that a Gospel writer connected to Jesus as a "prophecy" and then counting it as fulfilled is circular.` },
    { h: "Failures don't get counted", t: `The calculation ignores the prophecies that failed: the messianic kingdom, the rebuilt temple, world peace, the return within a generation (see [[failed-return]]), and Tyre (see [[tyre]]). Counting hits and ignoring misses is how fortune-tellers seem accurate.` }
  ],
  replies: [
    { q: "Even if you remove the weak ones, the remaining prophecies are still statistically impossible.", a: `The problem isn't only weak prophecies. Any event reported by biased sources who knew the prediction and wrote decades later can't be treated as a random draw. To get a meaningful probability, you need independent verification that the events happened *and* that the prophecies were predictions. We don't have that for any of them.`, k: "remove weak prophecies still statistically impossible remaining" }
  ],
  sources: [
    "Stoner, P. (1958). Science Speaks. Moody Press.",
    "McDowell, J. & McDowell, S. (2017). Evidence That Demands a Verdict, updated ed. Thomas Nelson.",
    "Carrier, R. (2012). Proving History: Bayes's Theorem and the Quest for the Historical Jesus. Prometheus, ch. on probabilistic reasoning.",
    "Hájek, A. 'Interpretations of Probability.' Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy."
  ],
  keys: "prophecy odds probability stoner science speaks 10^17 silver dollars texas statistically impossible chance fulfilled prophecy math",
  rel: ["messianic-prophecy", "failed-return", "tyre"]
},
{
  id: "isaiah-7-14",
  cat: "prophecy",
  title: "Isaiah 7:14 Predicted the Virgin Birth",
  who: "Matthew 1:22–23; traditional Christian interpretation",
  claim: `"Behold, a virgin shall conceive and bear a son, and shall call his name Immanuel" (Isaiah 7:14). Seven centuries in advance, Isaiah predicted that the Messiah would be born of a virgin, and Matthew records its fulfillment in Jesus.`,
  short: `The Hebrew word is *almah*, "young woman," not the specific word for virgin (*betulah*). The sign was given to King Ahaz around 735 BCE about a crisis of his own time: before the child is old enough to know right from wrong, the two kings threatening Judah will be gone (7:16), which happened within a few years when Assyria conquered them. Matthew used the Greek translation, which rendered *almah* as *parthenos*, and read it as a prophecy about Jesus.`,
  points: [
    { h: "The word", t: `*Almah* means a young woman of marriageable age. It is used of Rebekah before marriage (Genesis 24:43) and of young women generally (Song of Songs 6:8; Proverbs 30:19, "the way of a man with an *almah*"). Hebrew has a more specific word for a virgin, *betulah*, which Isaiah uses elsewhere (23:4, 12; 37:22; 62:5). The Greek Septuagint translated *almah* here as *parthenos*, which can mean "virgin," and Matthew quotes the Greek.` },
    { h: "The context is Ahaz's war", t: `In Isaiah 7, the kings of Aram (Rezin) and Israel (Pekah) are attacking Judah. Isaiah tells Ahaz not to fear: "the young woman" (the Hebrew has a definite article, so a specific woman is meant) is pregnant or about to be, and "before the child knows how to refuse the evil and choose the good, the land before whose two kings you are in dread will be deserted" (7:16). Assyria conquered Damascus in 732 BCE and devastated Israel. The sign was fulfilled in Ahaz's lifetime. A birth 700 years later would have been no sign to Ahaz at all.` },
    { h: "A parallel sign child follows", t: `Isaiah 8:1–4 immediately describes another sign child, Isaiah's own son Maher-shalal-hash-baz: "before the child knows how to call 'My father' or 'My mother,' the wealth of Damascus and the spoil of Samaria will be carried away by the king of Assyria." The pattern of naming children as signs about the Assyrian crisis is the context of 7:14.` },
    { h: "Not a messianic text in Judaism", t: `There's no evidence that any Jewish interpreter before Christianity read Isaiah 7:14 as a prophecy of the messiah or of a virgin birth. Matthew's use of it fits his practice of reading scripture through Jesus (see [[messianic-prophecy]]).` }
  ],
  replies: [
    { q: "The Jewish translators of the Septuagint used parthenos, so they understood it as virgin.", a: `*Parthenos* often means virgin but can mean a young woman more generally. In the Septuagint, Dinah is called a *parthenos* after she had been raped (Genesis 34:3). In any case, the translators were rendering a Hebrew text whose context is Ahaz's crisis. Even a "virgin" who conceives in the normal way after marriage would fit the Septuagint, with no miracle implied.`, k: "septuagint parthenos translators virgin understood" },
    { q: "A young woman having a baby is no sign. It has to be miraculous.", a: `The sign in 7:14–16 is the timing, not the conception: by the time this child is a few years old, the threat will be gone. Isaiah gives similar signs with ordinary births (8:1–4). A miraculous birth 700 years later couldn't be a sign to Ahaz, to whom Isaiah was speaking.`, k: "young woman baby no sign must be miraculous" }
  ],
  sources: [
    "Blenkinsopp, J. (2000). Isaiah 1–39. Anchor Bible. Doubleday.",
    "Brown, R. E. (1993). The Birth of the Messiah, rev. ed., pp. 143–153.",
    "Levine, A.-J. & Brettler, M. Z. (2020). The Bible With and Without Jesus, ch. on Isaiah 7:14.",
    "Wildberger, H. (1991). Isaiah 1–12. Continental Commentary. Fortress."
  ],
  keys: "isaiah 7:14 virgin shall conceive almah young woman immanuel betulah parthenos septuagint virgin birth prophecy ahaz",
  rel: ["virgin-birth", "messianic-prophecy", "nativity"]
},
{
  id: "isaiah-53",
  cat: "prophecy",
  title: "Isaiah 53 Describes Jesus' Death",
  who: "Acts 8:32–35; Christian apologetics; Michael Brown, The Real Kosher Jesus (2012)",
  claim: `Isaiah 52:13–53:12, written centuries before Jesus, describes a Servant who is "despised and rejected," "pierced for our transgressions," led "like a lamb to the slaughter," "numbered with the transgressors," buried with the rich, and who then sees life again. It reads like an eyewitness account of the crucifixion.`,
  short: `The Servant in Isaiah 40–55 is explicitly and repeatedly identified as Israel. Chapter 53 is the confession of the nations, astonished that the exiled, suffering people of Israel were vindicated. The passage never mentions a messiah, crucifixion, or resurrection, and parts don't fit Jesus (the servant "shall see offspring and prolong his days"). It fits Jesus because the Gospel writers and early Christians used it to interpret his death and to shape their narratives of it.`,
  points: [
    { h: "The servant is Israel", t: `"But you, Israel, my servant, Jacob, whom I have chosen" (41:8). "But now hear, O Jacob my servant, Israel whom I have chosen" (44:1). "You are my servant, Israel, in whom I will be glorified" (49:3). Isaiah 40–55 (Second Isaiah) was written during or just after the Babylonian exile, in the 6th century BCE, to comfort exiles. The servant songs describe Israel's suffering in exile and its vindication before the nations.` },
    { h: "The speakers are the nations", t: `Isaiah 52:15 says kings will be astonished at the servant; 53:1 begins their response, "Who has believed what we have heard?" The "we" who thought the servant was stricken by God for his own sins, and who now realize he suffered for "our" sins, are the nations and kings, astonished at Israel's restoration. This reading is the traditional Jewish one, attested in Origen's time (*Against Celsus* 1.55).` },
    { h: "Details that don't fit Jesus", t: `"He shall see his offspring, and shall prolong his days" (53:10) doesn't fit Jesus, who had no children and died young. The Hebrew word for offspring (*zera*) normally means physical descendants. The servant is "a man of pains and acquainted with sickness" (53:3), but the Gospels don't portray Jesus as sick. The servant's grave is "with the wicked" and "with a rich man in his death" (53:9), but Jesus was crucified between criminals and buried by a rich man, which is how the Gospels arrange it, with Matthew adding that Joseph of Arimathea was rich (27:57).` },
    { h: "Not about a dying messiah", t: `The word "messiah" doesn't appear in Isaiah 53. The only person called "messiah" (anointed) in Second Isaiah is Cyrus, the Persian king (45:1). There is no evidence that mainstream Judaism before Jesus expected a messiah who would suffer and die for sins.` }
  ],
  replies: [
    { q: "Israel can't be the servant because the servant is sinless, and Israel isn't.", a: `The text doesn't claim the servant is sinless. It says the servant suffered beyond what his own sins deserved, for the sins of others ("he was wounded for our transgressions"), which is Second Isaiah's theology of exile: Israel has "received from the Lord's hand double for all her sins" (40:2). The idea is that Israel suffered more than its share, for the benefit of the nations.`, k: "israel can't be servant sinless innocent no violence" }
  ],
  sources: [
    "Blenkinsopp, J. (2002). Isaiah 40–55. Anchor Bible. Doubleday.",
    "Levine, A.-J. & Brettler, M. Z. (2020). The Bible With and Without Jesus, ch. 8.",
    "Janowski, B. & Stuhlmacher, P. (eds.) (2004). The Suffering Servant: Isaiah 53 in Jewish and Christian Sources. Eerdmans.",
    "Origen. Against Celsus 1.55."
  ],
  keys: "isaiah 53 suffering servant pierced for our transgressions lamb to the slaughter despised rejected prophecy crucifixion servant israel",
  rel: ["messianic-prophecy", "atonement", "psalm-22"]
},
{
  id: "psalm-22",
  cat: "prophecy",
  title: "Psalm 22 Predicted the Crucifixion",
  who: "Christian apologetics; Mark 15:34; John 19:24",
  claim: `Psalm 22, written by David a thousand years before Jesus, begins "My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?", Jesus' words on the cross. It describes mockers, the sufferer's bones out of joint, "they pierced my hands and my feet," and soldiers casting lots for his clothing, all written before crucifixion was invented.`,
  short: `Psalm 22 is a psalm of lament by someone in distress who is rescued (verses 21–31 are thanksgiving for deliverance). The Hebrew of verse 16 most likely reads "like a lion, my hands and my feet," not "they pierced." The Gospel writers didn't watch these details fulfilled. They built their passion narratives using the psalm: Mark has Jesus quote verse 1, Matthew adds mockers quoting verse 8, and John adds the soldiers dividing clothes to match verse 18.`,
  points: [
    { h: "'Like a lion'", t: `The Masoretic Text of Psalm 22:16 (22:17 in Hebrew numbering) reads *ka'ari yadai ve-raglai*, "like a lion, my hands and my feet," continuing the image of animals surrounding the speaker (dogs, bulls, lions; verses 12–21). The Septuagint has "they dug" (*oryxan*), and one fragmentary Dead Sea manuscript (5/6HevPs) may read a verb (*ka'aru*). Even if a verb is original, its meaning is uncertain ("bound," "dug," or "shriveled"), and it isn't the ordinary word for piercing. The textual question is genuinely disputed. A disputed word is weak evidence for a prophecy.` },
    { h: "The Gospels are built from the psalm", t: `Mark has Jesus cry out Psalm 22:1 in Aramaic. Matthew 27:43 has the chief priests mock him with words from Psalm 22:8 ("He trusts in God; let God deliver him"), which is an unlikely thing for priests to say and fits Matthew's practice of adding fulfillments. John 19:23–24 describes soldiers dividing his garments and casting lots for his tunic, "to fulfill the scripture," explicitly quoting 22:18. The narratives were shaped to echo the psalm, so the "match" is by design.` },
    { h: "A psalm of deliverance", t: `The psalm ends with the sufferer saved: "you have rescued me from the horns of the wild oxen" (22:21) and praising God in the congregation. It's a model prayer of lament and thanksgiving used in worship, like many other individual laments (Psalms 13, 69, 88). Nothing in it mentions a messiah, crucifixion, or resurrection.` },
    { h: "'Before crucifixion was invented'", t: `This claim is irrelevant if verse 16 doesn't describe piercing. In any case, the psalm doesn't describe crucifixion: no cross, no nails, no hanging. "Bones out of joint," "my heart melts like wax," and "my tongue sticks to my jaws" are standard images of distress and weakness in Hebrew poetry.` }
  ],
  replies: [
    { q: "The Dead Sea Scrolls prove the original said 'pierced.'", a: `The relevant Qumran-era fragment (5/6HevPs) is damaged and has been read as *ka'aru*, a verb whose meaning is uncertain. It isn't the normal Hebrew word for "pierce" (*daqar*, used in Zechariah 12:10). Even granting a verb, the psalm describes being attacked by animals, and the details fit a lament, not a predicted execution. The broader point stands: the Gospels' passion narratives were shaped from the psalm.`, k: "dead sea scrolls pierced psalm 22 karu kaari lion" }
  ],
  sources: [
    "Kraus, H.-J. (1988). Psalms 1–59. Continental Commentary. Fortress.",
    "Swenson, K. (2004). 'Psalm 22:17: Circling around the Problem Again.' Journal of Biblical Literature 123(4).",
    "Brown, R. E. (1994). The Death of the Messiah, vol. 2. Doubleday.",
    "Crossan, J. D. (1995). Who Killed Jesus?, ch. 1."
  ],
  keys: "psalm 22 pierced my hands and feet my god why have you forsaken me cast lots crucifixion prophecy ka'ari lion",
  rel: ["messianic-prophecy", "isaiah-53"]
},
{
  id: "micah-5-2",
  cat: "prophecy",
  title: "Micah 5:2 Predicted Jesus' Birth in Bethlehem",
  who: "Matthew 2:5–6; Christian apologetics",
  claim: `"But you, O Bethlehem Ephrathah, who are too little to be among the clans of Judah, from you shall come forth for me one who is to be ruler in Israel, whose coming forth is from of old, from ancient days" (Micah 5:2). The prophet named the exact town of the Messiah's birth seven centuries in advance.`,
  short: `Micah is saying a new ruler will come from David's line (David's family came from Bethlehem); it's not necessarily about the place of birth. Micah's ruler is a military deliverer who will defeat Assyria (5:5–6), which Jesus didn't do. And Jesus' birth in Bethlehem is reported only by Matthew and Luke, in mutually incompatible stories that look designed to bring a Nazarene to Bethlehem to fit this verse.`,
  points: [
    { h: "Bethlehem Ephrathah is a clan", t: `"Ephrathah" is the name of a clan in Judah (1 Chronicles 4:4; Ruth 1:2). "From you shall come forth" can mean "from your lineage." Micah is saying that a ruler will arise from David's origins, a hope for a renewed Davidic dynasty, not specifying the town of a future birth.` },
    { h: "The ruler fights Assyria", t: `Micah continues: "And he shall be the one of peace. When the Assyrian comes into our land and treads in our palaces, then we will raise against him seven shepherds... they shall rule the land of Assyria with the sword" (5:5–6). This is an 8th-century prophecy about deliverance from the Assyrian empire. It wasn't fulfilled by Jesus.` },
    { h: "The Bethlehem birth is doubtful", t: `Mark and John never say Jesus was born in Bethlehem. John 7:41–42 records a crowd objecting that the messiah should come from Bethlehem, not Galilee, and the narrator doesn't correct them. Matthew and Luke get Jesus to Bethlehem in incompatible ways: in Matthew, his parents live there and later move to Nazareth; in Luke, they live in Nazareth and travel to Bethlehem for a census that historians can't confirm (see [[nativity]]). Most historical Jesus scholars think Jesus was from Nazareth and that the Bethlehem birth was inferred from this verse.` }
  ],
  replies: [
    { q: "'Whose origins are from of old, from ancient days' shows the ruler is eternal, so divine.", a: `The Hebrew *mi-qedem, mi-yeme 'olam* means "from of old, from ancient times," a reference to the ancient roots of the Davidic line, which goes back centuries before Micah. The same phrase describes ordinary past times elsewhere (Amos 9:11, "rebuild it as in the days of old"; Micah 7:14, "as in the days of old").`, k: "origins from of old ancient days eternal divine everlasting" }
  ],
  sources: [
    "Hillers, D. (1984). Micah. Hermeneia. Fortress.",
    "Brown, R. E. (1993). The Birth of the Messiah, rev. ed.",
    "Sanders, E. P. (1993). The Historical Figure of Jesus. Penguin, ch. 6."
  ],
  keys: "micah 5:2 bethlehem ephrathah born in bethlehem prophecy ruler messiah birthplace",
  rel: ["nativity", "messianic-prophecy", "virgin-birth"]
},
{
  id: "zechariah",
  cat: "prophecy",
  title: "Zechariah Foretold the Details of the Passion",
  who: "Matthew 21:4–5 and 27:9–10; Mark 14:27; John 19:37; Josh McDowell's lists of fulfilled messianic prophecies",
  claim: `Zechariah, writing over 500 years before Jesus, foretold details of his last week. The king would come "lowly, and riding on a donkey" (9:9), fulfilled at the triumphal entry. He would be valued at thirty pieces of silver, thrown "to the potter, in Yahweh's house" (11:12–13), fulfilled when Judas returned the money and the priests bought the potter's field. "They will look to me whom they have pierced" (12:10) was fulfilled at the crucifixion, and "Strike the shepherd, and the sheep will be scattered" (13:7) when the disciples fled.`,
  short: `These are cases where the Gospel writers shaped their stories to match scripture, and sometimes we can watch them doing it. Matthew, misreading the poetic parallelism of Zechariah 9:9 ("on a donkey, even on a colt"), has Jesus ride two animals at once, while Mark has one. Matthew attributes the thirty pieces of silver to Jeremiah, and Acts tells a different story about Judas's death and the field. In context, Zechariah's silver is the prophet's own wages in an allegory about rejected leadership; the "pierced" one is mourned during a war in which Jerusalem defeats the nations; and the struck shepherd is followed by the death of two-thirds of the land. And any messianic claimant could choose to ride a donkey into Jerusalem.`,
  points: [
    { h: "One animal or two", t: `Zechariah 9:9 says the king comes "riding on a donkey, even on a colt, the foal of a donkey," which is Hebrew parallelism: one animal, described twice. Mark (11:2–7) and Luke have one colt. Matthew has the disciples bring "the donkey and the colt," and Jesus "sat on them" (21:7). Matthew seems to have read the prophecy literally and adjusted the story to match, which shows which way the influence ran.` },
    { h: "Thirty pieces of silver", t: `In Zechariah 11, the prophet plays a shepherd whose employers pay him thirty pieces of silver, an insultingly small wage, and Yahweh tells him to throw it "to the potter" in the Temple. Matthew 27:9 attributes the passage to Jeremiah, an error that some copyists tried to correct. And Acts 1:18 has Judas buy a field himself and die "falling headlong," while in Matthew he hangs himself and the priests buy the field (see [[contradictions]]). Two different stories about the money suggest the details grew out of scripture rather than memory.` },
    { h: "The pierced one and the shepherd", t: `Zechariah 12 describes a future battle in which Yahweh destroys the nations attacking Jerusalem, after which the city mourns someone "whom they have pierced," a figure whose identity is debated. In Zechariah 13:7–8, Yahweh strikes his shepherd and then "two parts" of the land "will be cut off and die." Lifting one phrase from each passage and applying it to Jesus ignores everything around it.` },
    { h: "How the method worked", t: `Early Christians read scripture as a code about their own time, like the authors of the Dead Sea Scrolls, whose commentaries (*pesharim*) applied the prophets line by line to their own community and its Teacher of Righteousness. Many scholars think some details of the passion are "prophecy historicized": told in order to fulfill texts, rather than texts that happened to be fulfilled (Crossan, 1995). Either way, fulfillments created by the people telling the story aren't evidence.` }
  ],
  replies: [],
  sources: [
    "Meyers, C. L. & Meyers, E. M. (1993). Zechariah 9–14. Anchor Bible 25C. Doubleday.",
    "Brown, R. E. (1994). The Death of the Messiah, vol. 1. Doubleday.",
    "Crossan, J. D. (1995). Who Killed Jesus? HarperSanFrancisco.",
    "Petersen, D. L. (1995). Zechariah 9–14 and Malachi. Westminster John Knox.",
    "Lindars, B. (1961). New Testament Apologetic. SCM."
  ],
  keys: "zechariah prophecy donkey triumphal entry thirty pieces of silver potter's field pierced zechariah 12 10 strike the shepherd sheep scattered zechariah fulfilled",
  rel: ["messianic-prophecy", "psalm-22", "contradictions", "prophecy-odds"]
},
{
  id: "daniel-9",
  cat: "prophecy",
  title: "Daniel's Seventy Weeks Predicted the Date of the Messiah",
  who: "Sir Robert Anderson, The Coming Prince (1895); Harold Hoehner, Chronological Aspects of the Life of Christ (1977); Josh McDowell",
  claim: `Daniel 9:24–27 predicts that from "the word to restore and rebuild Jerusalem" until "an anointed one, a prince," will be 7 weeks and 62 weeks, that is, 483 years. Counting from Artaxerxes' decree in 445 BCE using 360-day "prophetic years" leads exactly to Jesus' entry into Jerusalem in 32 or 33 CE. After this, the "anointed one shall be cut off," which is the crucifixion, and the city and sanctuary are destroyed, which happened in 70 CE.`,
  short: `Daniel was written around 165 BCE (see [[daniel]]), and its seventy weeks reinterpret Jeremiah's 70-year prophecy for the crisis under Antiochus IV. The "anointed one cut off" is the high priest Onias III, murdered in 171 BCE. The Christian calculations work only by choosing among several possible decrees, switching to 360-day years, and inserting a 2,000-year gap between the 69th and 70th weeks. That flexibility guarantees a fit.`,
  points: [
    { h: "Context: Jeremiah's 70 years", t: `Daniel 9 opens with Daniel puzzling over Jeremiah's prophecy that the exile would last 70 years (Jeremiah 25:11–12; 29:10). The angel Gabriel explains it as 70 "weeks" of years, 490 years, ending with the restoration of the sanctuary and an end to sin. The author is living in the persecution of Antiochus IV (167–164 BCE), and the 70th week is that crisis.` },
    { h: "Onias III and Antiochus", t: `"An anointed one shall be cut off and shall have nothing" (9:26) best fits the murder of the legitimate high priest Onias III (2 Maccabees 4:33–35, about 171 BCE). The "prince who is to come" who destroys the city and sanctuary, makes a covenant "for one week," and in the middle of it stops sacrifice and sets up "an abomination that desolates" (9:27) is Antiochus, who desecrated the Temple in 167 BCE. The same events appear in Daniel 8 and 11. Collins's Hermeneia commentary sets out this consensus.` },
    { h: "The calculation is adjustable", t: `Candidate starting points include Jeremiah's word (587 BCE), Cyrus's decree (538), Artaxerxes' decree to Ezra (458/457), and the commission to Nehemiah (445/444). Calculators can use solar years or 360-day "prophetic years," and end at Jesus' baptism, his entry to Jerusalem, or his death. The dispensationalist reading also places a gap of 2,000 years and counting between the 69th and 70th weeks, though the text presents 70 continuous weeks. With this many free parameters, some combination will always fit.` },
    { h: "The 70th week didn't bring the end", t: `Daniel 9:24 says the 70 weeks would "finish the transgression, put an end to sin, atone for iniquity, bring in everlasting righteousness, seal vision and prophet, and anoint a most holy place." That didn't happen in 164 BCE or in the 1st century CE. The gap theory postpones the unfulfilled parts indefinitely.` }
  ],
  replies: [
    { q: "The 483 years land exactly on Palm Sunday, AD 32. That can't be coincidence.", a: `Anderson's calculation starts from one chosen decree, converts to 360-day years, and lands on a date for the crucifixion (AD 32) that most chronologists reject (30 or 33 CE are more common). Different apologists use different starting points and end points, each claiming precision. When the same prophecy is said to fit several different dates precisely, the precision is in the method, not the prophecy.`, k: "483 years palm sunday ad 32 anderson 360 day years exact" }
  ],
  sources: [
    "Collins, J. J. (1993). Daniel. Hermeneia. Fortress, pp. 343–360.",
    "Anderson, R. (1895). The Coming Prince. Hodder & Stoughton.",
    "Hoehner, H. (1977). Chronological Aspects of the Life of Christ. Zondervan.",
    "Grabbe, L. (1997). 'The 70-Weeks Prophecy (Daniel 9:24–27) in Early Jewish Interpretation.' In Evans & Talmon (eds.), The Quest for Context and Meaning. Brill."
  ],
  keys: "daniel 9 seventy weeks 70 weeks 483 years anointed one cut off messiah date prophecy artaxerxes decree palm sunday",
  rel: ["daniel", "messianic-prophecy", "prophecy-odds"]
},
{
  id: "messiah-before-70",
  cat: "prophecy",
  title: "The Messiah Had to Come Before the Temple Fell",
  who: "Josh McDowell, The New Evidence That Demands a Verdict (1999); Michael Brown, Answering Jewish Objections to Jesus, vol. 3 (2003); Messianic Jewish apologetics",
  claim: `Several prophecies require the Messiah to arrive while the Second Temple was standing. Daniel 9:26 says "the Anointed One will be cut off" before "the city and the sanctuary" are destroyed. Haggai 2:9 promises that "the latter glory of this house will be greater than the former," and Malachi 3:1 that "the Lord, whom you seek, will suddenly come to his temple." Genesis 49:10 says the scepter won't depart from Judah until the one "to whom it belongs" comes. The Temple was destroyed in 70 CE, so the Messiah must have come before then, and Jesus is the only credible candidate.`,
  short: `None of these texts gives a timetable for a messiah. Daniel 9 refers to events of the 2nd century BCE: the "Anointed One" who is "cut off" is best identified with the high priest Onias III, murdered around 171 BCE, and the desolation is Antiochus IV's profanation of the Temple (see [[daniel-9]]). Haggai's hopes were pinned on Zerubbabel, the governor of Judah, whom God promised to make his "signet" while overthrowing "the throne of kingdoms," and that didn't happen. Malachi describes Yahweh himself coming in judgment, preceded by Elijah. Genesis 49:10 is a tribal blessing whose key phrase is obscure, and Judah's "scepter," in any political sense, departed in 586 BCE. Jesus wasn't the only claimant either, and he didn't do what the prophets expected the Messiah to do.`,
  points: [
    { h: "Haggai's candidate was Zerubbabel", t: `Haggai 2:21–23 tells Zerubbabel, "governor of Judah," that Yahweh "will overthrow the throne of kingdoms" and "will make you as a signet, for I have chosen you." Haggai and Zechariah expected the rebuilt Temple and its Davidic governor to begin a new age, with "the precious things of all nations" flowing in (Haggai 2:7). Zerubbabel then disappears from the historical record. This is a failed expectation, not a prediction about Jesus.` },
    { h: "Malachi's coming 'Lord'", t: `Malachi 3:1–5 describes Yahweh coming to his Temple to purify the priests and to judge sorcerers, adulterers, and oppressors, after a messenger later identified as Elijah (4:5). It doesn't concern a human messiah, and "the Lord... will suddenly come to his temple" sets no date.` },
    { h: "The scepter", t: `Genesis 49:10 is part of Jacob's blessings on the tribes, which most scholars date to the monarchy. Its key phrase is notoriously obscure, and is often rendered "until Shiloh comes." Read as a political prophecy, it failed: Judah lost its king in 586 BCE, and the Hasmonean rulers of the 2nd and 1st centuries BCE were a priestly family, not descendants of Judah. Apologists have to redefine the scepter to make the timing work.` },
    { h: "Many claimants, one job description", t: `Josephus names several would-be deliverers in the 1st century, and in 132 CE Rabbi Akiva hailed Simon bar Kosiba (Bar Kokhba) as the messiah. Jewish expectation, drawn from the prophets, was that the messiah would restore Israel's independence, rebuild the Temple, gather the exiles, and bring universal peace, when nations "shall beat their swords into plowshares" (Isaiah 2:4; see also Isaiah 11; Ezekiel 37). Jesus did none of these things during his life, which is why Christians developed the idea of a second coming.` }
  ],
  replies: [],
  sources: [
    "Collins, J. J. (1993). Daniel. Hermeneia. Fortress.",
    "Meyers, C. L. & Meyers, E. M. (1987). Haggai, Zechariah 1–8. Anchor Bible 25B. Doubleday.",
    "Westermann, C. (1986). Genesis 37–50. Augsburg.",
    "Horsley, R. A. & Hanson, J. S. (1985). Bandits, Prophets, and Messiahs. Winston Press.",
    "Brown, M. (2003). Answering Jewish Objections to Jesus, vol. 3. Baker.",
    "Schäfer, P. (ed.) (2003). The Bar Kokhba War Reconsidered. Mohr Siebeck."
  ],
  keys: "messiah must come before 70 ad temple destroyed daniel 9 26 haggai 2 9 malachi 3 1 scepter genesis 49 10 shiloh messiah timing second temple",
  rel: ["daniel-9", "messianic-prophecy", "temple-prediction", "failed-return"]
},
{
  id: "resurrection-foretold",
  cat: "prophecy",
  title: "The Scriptures Foretold the Messiah's Resurrection",
  who: "Acts 2:25–31 and 13:35 (Peter and Paul on Psalm 16); 1 Corinthians 15:4 ('raised on the third day according to the Scriptures'); Matthew 12:40 (the sign of Jonah); Hosea 6:2",
  claim: `Paul says Jesus was "raised on the third day according to the Scriptures" (1 Corinthians 15:4), and the scriptures he had in mind are clear. David wrote, "you will not leave my soul in Sheol, neither will you allow your holy one to see corruption" (Psalm 16:10), which Peter applied to Jesus, since David's own body did decay (Acts 2:29–31). Hosea foretold the third day: "On the third day he will raise us up" (6:2). Jesus himself pointed to Jonah's three days in the great fish (Matthew 12:40). And Isaiah 53 says that after the servant's death, "he will see his offspring. He will prolong his days."`,
  short: `Psalm 16 is a prayer by someone asking to be kept *from* death, not raised after it: the psalmist trusts that God won't abandon him to Sheol, the grave. Peter's argument in Acts depends on the Greek translation's word for "decay," a meaning the Hebrew doesn't clearly carry. Hosea 6:1–3 is a song of national repentance, which God immediately dismisses as fleeting "like the dew that disappears early" (6:4). Matthew's "three days and three nights" doesn't fit a Friday-to-Sunday burial, and Luke's version of the sign of Jonah is about preaching, not a fish. The servant of Isaiah 53 is identified in the surrounding chapters as Israel (see [[isaiah-53]]). Early Christians found these readings after the fact, as they searched their scriptures to make sense of the crucifixion and the resurrection experiences.`,
  points: [
    { h: "Psalm 16 in context", t: `In Hebrew poetry, being kept from Sheol and "the pit" means being kept alive, as in many psalms of deliverance: "you have brought up my soul from Sheol. You have kept me alive, that I should not go down to the pit" (30:3). Acts quotes the Greek Septuagint, whose word *diaphthora* means "decay," and builds an argument on it that the Hebrew doesn't support.` },
    { h: "Hosea 6", t: `"After two days he will revive us. On the third day he will raise us up" (6:2) is spoken by the people of Israel about their own recovery after punishment ("he has torn us to pieces, and he will heal us"). God's reply follows at once: "your love is like a morning cloud, and like the dew that disappears early" (6:4). It's a rejected song of repentance by a nation, not a prediction that an individual would rise.` },
    { h: "The sign of Jonah", t: `Matthew 12:40 says the Son of Man "will be three days and three nights in the heart of the earth," but the Gospels have Jesus buried on Friday evening and risen by early Sunday, about 36 hours at most. In Luke 11:29–30, the sign of Jonah is Jonah's preaching to the Ninevites, with no mention of the fish. Matthew's version looks like an expansion of the saying.` },
    { h: "Found after the fact", t: `The Gospels say the disciples didn't expect a resurrection: "as yet they didn't know the Scripture, that he must rise from the dead" (John 20:9), and the women's report "seemed to them to be nonsense" (Luke 24:11). The proofs from scripture were assembled afterward. Movements in crisis regularly search their scriptures for explanations. After the "Great Disappointment" of 1844, Millerites reinterpreted Daniel 8:14 as referring to an event in heaven, and Seventh-day Adventism grew from that reinterpretation (see [[dying-messiah]]).` }
  ],
  replies: [],
  sources: [
    "Juel, D. (1988). Messianic Exegesis: Christological Interpretation of the Old Testament in Early Christianity. Fortress.",
    "Lindars, B. (1961). New Testament Apologetic. SCM.",
    "Wolff, H. W. (1974). Hosea. Hermeneia. Fortress.",
    "Numbers, R. L. & Butler, J. M. (eds.) (1987). The Disappointed: Millerism and Millenarianism in the Nineteenth Century. Indiana University Press.",
    "Festinger, L., Riecken, H. & Schachter, S. (1956). When Prophecy Fails. University of Minnesota Press."
  ],
  keys: "resurrection prophesied old testament psalm 16 holy one see decay hosea 6 2 third day sign of jonah three days three nights according to the scriptures predicted resurrection",
  rel: ["isaiah-53", "dying-messiah", "messianic-prophecy", "passion-predictions"]
},
{
  id: "cyrus",
  cat: "prophecy",
  title: "Isaiah Named Cyrus 150 Years in Advance",
  who: "Traditional authorship of Isaiah; Josephus, Antiquities 11.1–7; Josh McDowell; Norman Geisler, Baker Encyclopedia of Christian Apologetics (1999)",
  claim: `Isaiah of Jerusalem, writing in the 8th century BCE, named the Persian king Cyrus as the one who would rebuild Jerusalem. Yahweh "says of Cyrus, 'He is my shepherd, and shall perform all my pleasure,' even saying of Jerusalem, 'She will be built'" (44:28), and calls him "his anointed" (45:1). Cyrus conquered Babylon in 539 BCE and allowed the Jewish exiles to return and rebuild the Temple, more than 150 years after Isaiah. Josephus reports that Cyrus read the prophecy about himself and was moved to fulfill it. Naming a king before his birth is impossible without divine foreknowledge.`,
  short: `Critical scholars, including many Christians, recognize that Isaiah 40–55 was written by an anonymous prophet during the Babylonian exile, around 540 BCE, when Cyrus was already conquering the region. These chapters address exiles in Babylon as their present audience, speak of Jerusalem as already in ruins, and never mention Isaiah, who appears repeatedly in chapters 1–39. The prophecy is precise about what had already happened, Cyrus's rise, and wrong about what hadn't: a glorious return in which the nations bow down to Israel. Josephus's story comes from the 1st century CE. And Cyrus's own propaganda, the Cyrus Cylinder, credits Babylon's god Marduk with choosing him in very similar terms.`,
  points: [
    { h: "Why scholars date Isaiah 40–55 to the exile", t: `The chapters speak to people in Babylon ("Comfort, comfort my people," 40:1), describe Jerusalem and the cities of Judah as "waste places" waiting to be rebuilt (44:26), and treat Babylon's fall as imminent. They differ in style, vocabulary, and theology from Isaiah 1–39, and the 8th-century prophet, named repeatedly in those chapters, is never mentioned. Scholars in the late 18th century developed the theory of a "Second Isaiah" from these features of the text.` },
    { h: "Accurate where hindsight helps, wrong where it doesn't", t: `The prophecies about Cyrus fit the time when he was already campaigning. The prophecies about what would follow didn't come true: a highway through the desert and a transformed landscape (40:3–5; 41:18–19), kings bowing down to Israel to "lick the dust of your feet" (49:23), and a Jerusalem never again entered by "the uncircumcised and the unclean" (52:1). The actual return was small and difficult, as Haggai, Ezra, and Nehemiah show, and the Temple wasn't rebuilt until 515 BCE, under Darius.` },
    { h: "Marduk said it too", t: `The Cyrus Cylinder, a Babylonian text written for Cyrus after he took Babylon, says that Marduk searched for a righteous ruler, called out the name of Cyrus, and let him enter Babylon without a battle. Each religion credited its own god with Cyrus. Second Isaiah's portrait of Cyrus as Yahweh's anointed fits the political theology of its time.` },
    { h: "Josephus's story", t: `Josephus wrote around 600 years after the events. His claim that Cyrus read Isaiah's prophecy comes from Jewish tradition, not Persian records, and nothing outside Josephus supports it.` }
  ],
  replies: [
    { q: "Dating Isaiah 40–55 late assumes predictive prophecy is impossible. That's circular.", a: `The late date rests on the text's own point of view: it addresses exiles, describes Jerusalem in ruins, and never mentions Isaiah. Scholars apply the same criteria to any text, religious or not. The prophecy claim isn't neutral either: it needs the 8th-century date to be established independently, and it isn't. The real test of prophecy comes in the predictions that go beyond Cyrus's early career, and those failed.`, k: "dating isaiah late assumes prophecy impossible anti supernatural circular deutero isaiah" }
  ],
  sources: [
    "Blenkinsopp, J. (2002). Isaiah 40–55. Anchor Bible 19A. Doubleday.",
    "Childs, B. S. (2001). Isaiah. Westminster John Knox.",
    "Finkel, I. (ed.) (2013). The Cyrus Cylinder: The Great Persian Edict from Babylon. I. B. Tauris.",
    "Kuhrt, A. (1983). 'The Cyrus Cylinder and Achaemenid Imperial Policy.' Journal for the Study of the Old Testament 25: 83–97.",
    "Josephus, Antiquities 11.1–7."
  ],
  keys: "isaiah named cyrus 150 years before cyrus prophecy isaiah 44 45 cyrus anointed rebuild jerusalem deutero isaiah second isaiah",
  rel: ["daniel", "messianic-prophecy", "babylon-nineveh", "prophecy-odds"]
},
{
  id: "tyre",
  cat: "prophecy",
  title: "The Prophecy Against Tyre Was Fulfilled",
  who: "Josh McDowell, Evidence That Demands a Verdict; Ezekiel 26",
  claim: `Ezekiel 26 predicted that many nations would come against Tyre, that its walls would be destroyed, that its rubble would be thrown into the sea, that it would become a bare rock for drying fishing nets, and that it would never be rebuilt. Nebuchadnezzar destroyed the mainland city, and Alexander the Great later used its rubble to build a causeway to the island city in 332 BCE, exactly as foretold.`,
  short: `Ezekiel specifically says *Nebuchadnezzar* will break down Tyre's walls, plunder it, and destroy it (26:7–12). Nebuchadnezzar besieged Tyre for 13 years and failed to take it. Ezekiel himself admits this later: Ezekiel 29:17–20 says Nebuchadnezzar and his army "got nothing" for their labor against Tyre, so God would give him Egypt instead. Tyre was rebuilt after Alexander and has been inhabited ever since. Paul visited it (Acts 21:3–7), and today it is a city of tens of thousands.`,
  points: [
    { h: "Ezekiel names Nebuchadnezzar", t: `"I will bring against Tyre from the north Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon... He will kill your daughters on the mainland... He will set his battering rams against your walls... With the hoofs of his horses he will trample all your streets... *They* will plunder your riches" (26:7–12). The change from "he" to "they" in verse 12 doesn't change the subject of the oracle. Apologists who attribute the destruction to Alexander, 250 years later, have to separate verses the text keeps together.` },
    { h: "Ezekiel admits the failure", t: `In an oracle dated 571 BCE: "Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon made his army labor hard against Tyre. Every head was made bald, and every shoulder was rubbed bare, yet neither he nor his army got anything from Tyre to pay for the labor... Therefore I will give the land of Egypt to Nebuchadnezzar" (29:18–19). The Bible itself records that the prophecy didn't come true as stated.` },
    { h: "'You shall never be rebuilt'", t: `"You shall never be rebuilt" (26:14); "you shall be no more; though you are sought for, you will never be found again" (26:21). Tyre recovered after Nebuchadnezzar, was rebuilt after Alexander, flourished under the Romans, appears in the New Testament (Mark 7:24; Acts 21:3–7), and is now a city in southern Lebanon.` },
    { h: "The Egypt prophecy failed too", t: `Ezekiel 29:9–12 says Egypt would be made desolate and uninhabited for 40 years, with Egyptians scattered among the nations. That never happened. Nebuchadnezzar campaigned against Egypt around 568 BCE but didn't conquer or depopulate it. A prophet whose detailed predictions fail is the case described in Deuteronomy 18:22.` }
  ],
  replies: [
    { q: "The prophecy said 'many nations' would come. Nebuchadnezzar began it and Alexander finished it.", a: `Ezekiel 26:3 says "many nations," but verses 7–11 attribute the specific destruction to Nebuchadnezzar. And even if Alexander fulfilled part of it, the city was rebuilt and still exists, contrary to 26:14 and 26:21. Ezekiel himself says Nebuchadnezzar got nothing from Tyre (29:18).`, k: "many nations alexander finished nebuchadnezzar began waves" },
    { q: "The ancient mainland city was never rebuilt. Modern Tyre is in a different spot.", a: `Tyre's main city was always the island, which is where the modern city's old quarter stands, now connected to land by the silted-up causeway. The city was rebuilt and inhabited after Alexander. Shifting the prophecy's target to "the mainland suburb" is redefinition after the fact.`, k: "mainland city never rebuilt modern tyre different spot fishing nets" }
  ],
  sources: [
    "Ezekiel 26:1–21; 29:17–20.",
    "Zimmerli, W. (1983). Ezekiel 2. Hermeneia. Fortress.",
    "Katzenstein, H. J. (1973). The History of Tyre. Schocken Institute.",
    "Arrian. Anabasis of Alexander 2.15–24.",
    "Josephus. Against Apion 1.156 (on the 13-year siege by Nebuchadnezzar)."
  ],
  keys: "tyre prophecy ezekiel 26 nebuchadnezzar alexander the great fishing nets never rebuilt bare rock fulfilled prophecy",
  rel: ["prophecy-odds", "messianic-prophecy"]
},
{
  id: "babylon-nineveh",
  cat: "prophecy",
  title: "The Prophecies Against Babylon and Nineveh Came True",
  who: "Josh McDowell, Evidence That Demands a Verdict (1972); Peter Stoner, Science Speaks (1958); Hank Hanegraaff",
  claim: `The prophets foretold the fate of the great empires. Isaiah declared that Babylon "will never be inhabited... The Arabian will not pitch a tent there" (13:20), and Jeremiah that it would become "heaps... without inhabitant" (51:37). Today Babylon is a ruin. Nahum foretold the destruction of Nineveh, the mighty Assyrian capital, which fell in 612 BCE and was never rebuilt. Prophecies of the total and permanent destruction of the world's greatest cities, fulfilled to the letter, show divine foreknowledge.`,
  short: `Where the details are specific, they're wrong. Isaiah 13 predicts that the Medes would slaughter Babylon's young men and children. In fact, Babylon surrendered to the Persian Cyrus in 539 BCE with little violence and remained a major city for centuries; Alexander the Great died there in 323 BCE, and it declined only gradually after the Seleucid kings founded a new capital nearby. Most great ancient cities eventually became ruins, so predicting that an enemy capital will someday fall is a safe bet. Nahum was most likely written shortly before Nineveh fell, when Assyria was already collapsing. And prophecies against other nations that didn't come true, such as Ezekiel's forty-year desolation of Egypt, are rarely mentioned.`,
  points: [
    { h: "Babylon's actual history", t: `Isaiah 13:17–18 says the Medes' "bows will dash the young men in pieces" and their "eyes will not spare children," and 13:19 compares Babylon's fate to Sodom and Gomorrah. Instead, Babylon opened its gates to Cyrus in 539 BCE, stayed an important city under the Persians, served Alexander as a capital, and declined over several centuries after Seleucus I founded Seleucia on the Tigris. Smaller-scale settlement continued long afterward. The site is now surrounded by modern towns and was inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2019.` },
    { h: "Every city falls eventually", t: `Ur, Uruk, Nineveh, Troy, Mycenae, and Persepolis all became ruins. Predicting that an enemy's capital will one day be desolate succeeds very often, especially when no deadline is given. The real test lies in the specific, time-bound details, and those failed (see [[prophecy-odds]]).` },
    { h: "Nahum's timing", t: `Nahum refers to the sack of Thebes (No-Amon, 3:8) as a past event, and that happened in 663 BCE. So the book was written between 663 and Nineveh's fall in 612, and most scholars place it near the end of that period, when Assyria was collapsing under attacks by the Babylonians and the Medes. Predicting the fall of a crumbling empire's capital is political commentary, not foreknowledge.` },
    { h: "The prophecies that failed", t: `Ezekiel predicted that Egypt would be "an utter waste and desolation," uninhabited for forty years, with its people scattered among the nations (29:10–12). That never happened. He predicted Tyre would become "a bare rock" that would "be built no more" (26:14), and later conceded that Nebuchadnezzar's army "had no wages" from its siege of Tyre (29:18; see [[tyre]]). A method that counts the hits and forgets the misses will find fulfilled prophecy anywhere.` }
  ],
  replies: [],
  sources: [
    "Seymour, M. (2014). Babylon: Legend, History and the Ancient City. I. B. Tauris.",
    "Boiy, T. (2004). Late Achaemenid and Hellenistic Babylon. Peeters.",
    "Kuhrt, A. (1995). The Ancient Near East, c. 3000–330 BC. Routledge.",
    "Roberts, J. J. M. (1991). Nahum, Habakkuk, and Zephaniah. Westminster John Knox.",
    "UNESCO World Heritage Centre (2019). 'Babylon.'"
  ],
  keys: "babylon never inhabited isaiah 13 jeremiah 51 prophecy fulfilled babylon ruins nineveh nahum fall of nineveh 612 prophecies against nations fulfilled",
  rel: ["tyre", "prophecy-odds", "cyrus", "israel-1948"]
},
{
  id: "israel-1948",
  cat: "prophecy",
  title: "Israel's Rebirth in 1948 Fulfilled Prophecy",
  who: "Dispensationalist apologetics; Hal Lindsey, The Late Great Planet Earth (1970); John Hagee",
  claim: `The Bible predicted that the scattered Jewish people would return to their land and Israel would be reborn as a nation: "Shall a land be born in one day? Shall a nation be brought forth in one moment?" (Isaiah 66:8; see also Ezekiel 37, the valley of dry bones). On May 14, 1948, it happened in a single day. No other ancient people has returned to its homeland after nearly 2,000 years.`,
  short: `The restoration prophecies in Isaiah, Jeremiah, and Ezekiel were about the return from the Babylonian exile, which happened in the 6th century BCE. They come packaged with a Davidic king, a rebuilt Temple, and universal peace, which didn't happen in 1948. Zionists who knew the Bible worked for decades to establish the state, so this "fulfillment" was partly self-fulfilling. And the prophecy industry around 1948 has a record of failed predictions, such as the end of the world by 1988.`,
  points: [
    { h: "Return from Babylon", t: `Ezekiel prophesied during the Babylonian exile (6th century BCE). His valley of dry bones (Ezekiel 37) is explained in the text itself: "these bones are the whole house of Israel... I will bring you home into the land of Israel" (37:11–12), followed by a reunited kingdom under "my servant David" (37:24) and a sanctuary in their midst forever (37:26–28). The exiles began returning under Cyrus in 538 BCE. Isaiah 66 concerns the same post-exilic hope.` },
    { h: "The package didn't arrive", t: `The restoration prophecies include a Davidic king ruling (Ezekiel 37:24), the Temple rebuilt with God present forever (37:26–28), the nations streaming to Jerusalem in peace (Isaiah 2:2–4), and the end of war. In 1948 there was a secular state, born in war, with no king and no temple. Claiming the part that happened and deferring the rest is selective.` },
    { h: "Self-fulfilling", t: `Zionism drew on biblical attachment to the land. Christian restorationists in Britain and America supported the return partly *because* they believed it was prophesied. The Balfour Declaration (1917) was influenced by such beliefs. When people who know a prophecy act to bring it about, its fulfillment isn't evidence of supernatural foresight.` },
    { h: "The prediction record", t: `Hal Lindsey's best-selling *The Late Great Planet Earth* (1970) suggested that the generation that saw Israel's rebirth would see the end, around 1988, since a biblical generation is about 40 years. Edgar Whisenant's *88 Reasons Why the Rapture Will Be in 1988* sold millions of copies. Similar predictions have been made and failed since the Millerites (1844). A method of interpretation that keeps making false predictions isn't reliable when it claims a hit.` }
  ],
  replies: [
    { q: "No other nation has been restored after 2,000 years of exile. It's unique.", a: `It's historically remarkable, but explicable: the Jewish community maintained a strong religious and cultural identity centered on the land, and a political movement deliberately worked toward a return. Jews also lived continuously in the land in smaller numbers throughout. Other peoples have revived languages and states (the revival of Hebrew has parallels in Irish and Estonian national revivals). A remarkable human achievement driven by a religious hope isn't evidence that the hope was divinely predicted.`, k: "no other nation restored 2000 years unique jews survived" }
  ],
  sources: [
    "Zimmerli, W. (1983). Ezekiel 2. Hermeneia. Fortress.",
    "Lindsey, H. (1970). The Late Great Planet Earth. Zondervan.",
    "Weber, T. (2004). On the Road to Armageddon: How Evangelicals Became Israel's Best Friend. Baker Academic.",
    "Lewis, D. (2010). The Origins of Christian Zionism. Cambridge University Press."
  ],
  keys: "israel 1948 rebirth nation born in a day isaiah 66:8 ezekiel 37 dry bones jews returned prophecy fulfilled end times",
  rel: ["failed-return", "prophecy-odds", "messianic-prophecy"]
},
{
  id: "temple-prediction",
  cat: "prophecy",
  title: "Jesus Predicted the Temple's Destruction",
  who: "Mark 13:1–2 and parallels; Luke 19:41–44 and 21:20–24; N. T. Wright, Jesus and the Victory of God (1996); Craig Evans",
  claim: `Looking at the Temple's huge stones, Jesus said, "There will not be left here one stone on another, which will not be thrown down" (Mark 13:2). About forty years later, in 70 CE, the Romans destroyed the Temple, just as Jesus said it would happen within "this generation." The prediction was precise, came true within the stated time, and was made when the Temple, newly rebuilt by Herod, seemed permanent.`,
  short: `If the Gospels were written around or after 70, as most scholars think, the details may have been shaped by hindsight, especially in Luke (see [[early-gospels]]). Even if Jesus did predict the destruction, that wouldn't require foreknowledge. Prophecies against the Temple were a recognized genre (Jeremiah made one about the First Temple), relations with Rome were tense, and Josephus describes another Jesus, Jesus son of Ananias, who cried woe against Jerusalem and the Temple for seven years before the war. And in Mark, the prediction is bundled with the end of the world: the sun darkened and the Son of Man coming in clouds, all within "this generation." The Temple fell. The rest didn't happen (see [[failed-return]]).`,
  points: [
    { h: "Another Jesus with the same message", t: `Josephus (*Jewish War* 6.300–309) reports that in 62 CE, a peasant named Jesus son of Ananias began crying out in the Temple against "Jerusalem and the holy house" and "this whole people." The authorities had him beaten and handed him over to the Roman governor, and he kept up his cry for seven years until a stone from a Roman siege engine killed him. Prophecies of the Temple's doom were in the air.` },
    { h: "Precedent", t: `Jeremiah declared that Yahweh would make the Temple "like Shiloh" (26:6), and the Babylonians destroyed the First Temple. For a prophet who believed God was about to judge Israel, predicting the Temple's fall was a natural part of the message. Many historians think Jesus did say something against the Temple, since it appears in the charges at his trial (Mark 14:58).` },
    { h: "Part of a package", t: `Mark 13 continues: "in those days... the sun will be darkened... Then they will see the Son of Man coming in clouds with great power and glory," and "this generation will not pass away until all these things happen" (13:24–30). If the Temple prophecy counts as fulfilled within that generation, the coming of the Son of Man within the same generation counts as failed.` },
    { h: "Not quite every stone", t: `Herod's massive retaining walls, including the Western Wall, still stand. Apologists answer that Jesus meant the Temple buildings, not the walls of the platform, which is fair. But it shows how readily the wording of a prophecy is adjusted to fit the outcome.` }
  ],
  replies: [],
  sources: [
    "Josephus, Jewish War 6.300–309.",
    "Allison, D. C. (1998). Jesus of Nazareth: Millenarian Prophet. Fortress.",
    "Kloppenborg, J. S. (2005). 'Evocatio Deorum and the Date of Mark.' Journal of Biblical Literature 124(3): 419–450.",
    "Theissen, G. (1991). The Gospels in Context. Fortress.",
    "Sanders, E. P. (1985). Jesus and Judaism. Fortress."
  ],
  keys: "jesus predicted temple destruction not one stone on another 70 ad temple destroyed fulfilled prophecy this generation mark 13 olivet discourse",
  rel: ["failed-return", "early-gospels", "messiah-before-70", "signs-of-times"]
},
{
  id: "failed-return",
  cat: "prophecy",
  title: "Jesus Will Return (and Never Promised a Date)",
  who: "Mainstream Christianity; preterists (N. T. Wright on the 'coming of the Son of Man'); 2 Peter 3:8–9",
  claim: `Jesus said no one knows the day or hour of his return (Mark 13:32). The early Christians lived in expectation but didn't set dates. Passages like "this generation will not pass away" refer to the destruction of Jerusalem in 70 CE (preterism) or to the "generation" that sees the final signs. "With the Lord one day is as a thousand years" (2 Peter 3:8). The delay shows God's patience.`,
  short: `The earliest Christian texts expect the end within their own lifetimes, and attribute that expectation to Jesus himself: "this generation will not pass away until all these things take place" (Mark 13:30), and "some standing here will not taste death until they see the kingdom of God come with power" (Mark 9:1). Paul expected to be alive at Jesus' return (1 Thessalonians 4:15–17). Most critical scholars, from Albert Schweitzer to Dale Allison (a Christian), conclude that Jesus was an apocalyptic prophet who expected the end imminently, and was wrong. C. S. Lewis called Mark 13:30 "the most embarrassing verse in the Bible."`,
  points: [
    { h: "Jesus' own words", t: `"Truly I tell you, there are some standing here who will not taste death until they see that the kingdom of God has come with power" (Mark 9:1). "Truly I tell you, this generation will not pass away until all these things have taken place" (Mark 13:30), right after describing the sun darkening, stars falling, and the Son of Man coming in clouds to gather the elect (13:24–27). "You will not have gone through all the towns of Israel before the Son of Man comes" (Matthew 10:23). To the high priest: "you will see the Son of Man seated at the right hand of the Power and coming with the clouds of heaven" (Mark 14:62).` },
    { h: "Paul expected to be alive", t: `"We who are alive, who are left until the coming of the Lord..." (1 Thessalonians 4:15, 17). "The appointed time has grown short; from now on, let even those who have wives be as though they had none... for the present form of this world is passing away" (1 Corinthians 7:29–31). Paul advised against marriage partly because the end was near. "We will not all die" (1 Corinthians 15:51). "Salvation is nearer to us now than when we became believers; the night is far gone, the day is near" (Romans 13:11–12).` },
    { h: "Every layer of the New Testament repeats it", t: `"The coming of the Lord is near" (James 5:8). "The end of all things is near" (1 Peter 4:7). "Children, it is the last hour" (1 John 2:18). "The time is near... Surely I am coming soon" (Revelation 1:3; 22:20). Then, in the latest NT book: "Where is the promise of his coming? For ever since our ancestors died, all things continue as they were" (2 Peter 3:4), answered with "with the Lord one day is like a thousand years" (3:8). That's a later author managing disappointment.` },
    { h: "The scholarly consensus", t: `Albert Schweitzer's *The Quest of the Historical Jesus* (1906) established the view of Jesus as an apocalyptic prophet expecting the imminent end. It remains the majority position (E. P. Sanders, 1985; Bart Ehrman, 1999; Paula Fredriksen, 1999; Dale Allison, 1998, 2010). Allison, a committed Christian, accepts that Jesus expected the end within his own generation and that the expectation was not fulfilled. C. S. Lewis wrote in "The World's Last Night" (1960) that Mark 13:30 "is certainly the most embarrassing verse in the Bible."` },
    { h: "Preterism doesn't work", t: `Preterists say Jesus' "coming" was fulfilled in the destruction of Jerusalem in 70 CE. But Mark 13:24–27 describes cosmic signs and the gathering of the elect from the ends of the earth, which didn't happen in 70 CE. Paul's expectation in 1 Thessalonians 4 is a bodily descent from heaven with the resurrection of the dead. Full preterism, which holds that the second coming has already happened, contradicts the creeds ("he will come again to judge the living and the dead"). Partial preterism keeps the creed but splits the same discourse into two events separated by millennia.` }
  ],
  replies: [
    { q: "'This generation' means the generation that sees the signs, or the Jewish race.", a: `In Mark, "this generation" (*he genea haute*) always means Jesus' contemporaries (8:12, 38; 9:19). And Mark 9:1 removes all ambiguity: "some standing *here* will not taste death." The redefinitions were invented because the plain reading failed.`, k: "this generation means race generation that sees signs genea" },
    { q: "A day is like a thousand years to the Lord (2 Peter 3:8).", a: `That's the excuse of a later author whose community was asking why Jesus hadn't returned (3:4). It shows the expectation was real and had failed. If "soon" can mean thousands of years, the word means nothing, and the early Christians, including Paul, who reorganized their lives around it, were misled.`, k: "day like thousand years 2 peter 3:8 god's time patience delay" },
    { q: "He meant the Transfiguration, which happened six days later.", a: `It would be odd to say "*some* of you will not die before" an event six days away. Mark 8:38–9:1 links the kingdom's coming with power to the Son of Man coming "in the glory of his Father with the holy angels," and Paul and the rest of the New Testament show the early church understood the promise as the return and the end.`, k: "transfiguration six days later some standing here" }
  ],
  sources: [
    "Schweitzer, A. (1906/2001). The Quest of the Historical Jesus. Fortress (first complete ed.).",
    "Allison, D. C. (1998). Jesus of Nazareth: Millenarian Prophet. Fortress.",
    "Allison, D. C. (2010). Constructing Jesus: Memory, Imagination, and History. Baker Academic.",
    "Ehrman, B. (1999). Jesus: Apocalyptic Prophet of the New Millennium. Oxford University Press.",
    "Sanders, E. P. (1985). Jesus and Judaism. Fortress.",
    "Lewis, C. S. (1960). 'The World's Last Night.' In The World's Last Night and Other Essays. Harcourt.",
    "Wright, N. T. (1996). Jesus and the Victory of God. Fortress (preterist-leaning reading)."
  ],
  keys: "second coming return of jesus this generation will not pass away some standing here will not taste death soon imminent end times apocalyptic prophet failed prophecy delay rapture",
  rel: ["historical-jesus", "daniel", "israel-1948", "prophecy-odds"]
},
{
  id: "signs-of-times",
  cat: "prophecy",
  title: "The Signs of the End Times Are Being Fulfilled Now",
  who: "Hal Lindsey, The Late Great Planet Earth (1970); Tim LaHaye & Jerry Jenkins, the Left Behind series (1995–2007); John Hagee; Matthew 24; 2 Timothy 3; Revelation 13",
  claim: `Jesus said the end would be preceded by "wars and rumors of wars" and "famines, plagues, and earthquakes in various places" (Matthew 24:6–7). Daniel foresaw that "many will run back and forth, and knowledge will be increased" (12:4), a description of modern travel and technology. Paul predicted that "in the last days," people would be "lovers of self, lovers of money... lovers of pleasure rather than lovers of God" (2 Timothy 3:1–4). Israel has been restored (see [[israel-1948]]), and Revelation's mark without which "no one would be able to buy or to sell" (13:17) is now technologically possible. These converging signs show that biblical prophecy is being fulfilled in our generation.`,
  short: `Every generation of Christians has believed these signs pointed to its own time, beginning with Paul, who wrote that "we who are alive" would be caught up to meet the Lord (1 Thessalonians 4:17). The signs are chosen so they can always be found: there are always wars, famines, earthquakes, and selfish people. They aren't even increasing: the number of large earthquakes per year has stayed roughly constant, and deaths from famine have fallen dramatically since the mid-20th century. Daniel 12:4 is about people searching a sealed book for its meaning, not about air travel. And the record of modern end-times predictions, from the Millerites in 1844 to Harold Camping in 2011, is one of complete failure.`,
  points: [
    { h: "A perennial expectation", t: `Paul expected to be alive at the Lord's return (1 Thessalonians 4:15–17). The 2nd-century Montanists expected the New Jerusalem to descend in Phrygia. The Anabaptists of Münster (1534–35), the Fifth Monarchy Men in 17th-century England, the Millerites (1844), the Jehovah's Witnesses (1914, 1925, 1975), Hal Lindsey (who implied the end would come within a generation of 1948), Edgar Whisenant (*88 Reasons Why the Rapture Will Be in 1988*), and Harold Camping (May 21, 2011) all saw the signs fulfilled in their own time (see [[failed-return]]).` },
    { h: "Earthquakes, famines, and wars", t: `The U.S. Geological Survey notes that the number of earthquakes of magnitude 7 or greater has remained fairly constant over the past century; the apparent increase comes from better detection and reporting. Famine deaths have fallen sharply since the 1960s (de Waal, *Mass Starvation*, 2018). Wars and human selfishness are constant features of history. Signs that are always present can't mark a particular time.` },
    { h: "Daniel 12:4", t: `"Shut up the words, and seal the book, even to the time of the end. Many will run back and forth, and knowledge will be increased." Daniel is told to seal his vision; many will search ("run back and forth") for its meaning, and understanding will grow. Reading it as a prophecy of jet travel and the internet means ignoring the first half of the verse.` },
    { h: "Unfalsifiable by design", t: `When a predicted date passes, the movement reinterprets the expectation rather than abandoning it (see [[dying-messiah]]). Because the signs are vague and the timetable is open, the claim that "we are in the last days" can be made in every generation and never be refuted, which means it predicts nothing (see [[unfalsifiable]]).` }
  ],
  replies: [],
  sources: [
    "Weber, E. (1999). Apocalypses: Prophecies, Cults, and Millennial Beliefs through the Ages. Harvard University Press.",
    "Boyer, P. (1992). When Time Shall Be No More: Prophecy Belief in Modern American Culture. Harvard University Press.",
    "Lindsey, H. (1970). The Late Great Planet Earth. Zondervan.",
    "U.S. Geological Survey. 'Why are we having so many earthquakes? Has naturally occurring earthquake activity been increasing?' Earthquake Hazards Program FAQ.",
    "de Waal, A. (2018). Mass Starvation: The History and Future of Famine. Polity."
  ],
  keys: "end times signs last days wars rumors of wars earthquakes increasing famines mark of the beast microchip knowledge increased daniel 12 4 rapture soon prophecy fulfilled today our generation",
  rel: ["failed-return", "israel-1948", "temple-prediction", "unfalsifiable"]
},
{
  id: "bible-codes",
  cat: "prophecy",
  title: "Hidden Codes in the Hebrew Bible Predict the Future",
  who: "Doron Witztum, Eliyahu Rips & Yoav Rosenberg, 'Equidistant Letter Sequences in the Book of Genesis,' Statistical Science (1994); Michael Drosnin, The Bible Code (1997); Grant Jeffrey, The Signature of God (1996)",
  claim: `When the Hebrew text of the Torah is searched for equidistant letter sequences, taking every nth letter, it reveals names and events clustered meaningfully together. In 1994, three Israeli researchers published a paper in the peer-reviewed journal *Statistical Science* showing that the names of famous rabbis appear in Genesis close to their dates of birth and death far more often than chance would allow. Michael Drosnin found Yitzhak Rabin's assassination encoded in the text and warned Rabin a year before it happened. Only a divine author could have encoded the future in the text.`,
  short: `In a large text searched with flexible rules, such patterns are guaranteed. In 1999, Brendan McKay, Dror Bar-Natan, Maya Bar-Hillel, and Gil Kalai published a rebuttal in the same journal. They showed that the 1994 result depended on choices about which names, spellings, and dates to use, and that similar choices produced equally "significant" results in a Hebrew translation of *War and Peace*. When Drosnin challenged critics to find a prime minister's assassination in *Moby-Dick*, McKay found Indira Gandhi's assassination there, among others. And a code written in individual letters couldn't survive transmission: the Dead Sea Scrolls show that the spelling of the Hebrew text varied widely before the standard Masoretic text was fixed.`,
  points: [
    { h: "The rebuttal in Statistical Science", t: `McKay and his colleagues showed that the 1994 experiment had many hidden choices: which rabbis to include, which of many possible name forms and date formats to search for, and how to measure closeness. Small changes to these choices, all defensible, made the effect disappear, and choices tuned the same way produced the effect in *War and Peace* (*Statistical Science* 14(2), 1999).` },
    { h: "Moby-Dick", t: `Drosnin told *Newsweek* in 1997, "When my critics find a message about the assassination of a prime minister encrypted in Moby Dick, I'll believe them." McKay then used Drosnin's methods to find "predictions" in *Moby-Dick* of the assassinations of Indira Gandhi, Martin Luther King Jr., and others.` },
    { h: "The text isn't stable letter by letter", t: `Hebrew words can be spelled with or without certain vowel letters, and manuscripts differ. The Dead Sea Scrolls, about a thousand years older than the earliest complete Masoretic manuscripts, show many such spelling differences (Tov, 2012). Any code that depends on the exact sequence of letters would be scrambled by ordinary copying.` },
    { h: "No track record", t: `Drosnin's warning to Rabin is his one celebrated hit, and with enough searching, some hits are inevitable. His books also warned of an atomic world war, which didn't come. The codes have never produced a specific, checkable prediction ahead of time that a skeptic couldn't match in a secular text.` }
  ],
  replies: [],
  sources: [
    "Witztum, D., Rips, E. & Rosenberg, Y. (1994). 'Equidistant Letter Sequences in the Book of Genesis.' Statistical Science 9(3): 429–438.",
    "McKay, B., Bar-Natan, D., Bar-Hillel, M. & Kalai, G. (1999). 'Solving the Bible Code Puzzle.' Statistical Science 14(2): 150–173.",
    "Drosnin, M. (1997). The Bible Code. Simon & Schuster.",
    "Tov, E. (2012). Textual Criticism of the Hebrew Bible, 3rd ed. Fortress."
  ],
  keys: "bible code hidden codes torah equidistant letter sequences els drosnin rabin assassination predicted statistical science witztum rips moby dick",
  rel: ["prophecy-odds", "unfalsifiable", "textual-variants"]
}
]);
